Scenarios — case, explanation, evidence

Six worked cases run against the reference model aisvs-c9-action-class-conformance at reversibility==1.2.0 (the release that closed four fail-open paths; a verdict recorded against an earlier release does not transfer — property 14).

Each case is written three times, for three readers:

The case — what the agent is asked to do, in one line.
What the model decides, and why — plain-language reading of the record.
The evidence — the raw decision record, in a fold, for anyone who wants to re-run it. The model is single-source of truth; the JSON is its output, not our claim.

The reference model already grades on two axes — reversibility and consequence, "oversight is the worse of the two" (property 5). This analysis is not "one axis vs two." It is about a third axis the model does not have: accountability — whether the agent can be the accountable subject of what the action creates. The author of the model states there is no such axis in C9.

The one-line result

Across every case, the only thing that ever lifts the gate to HUMAN_OWNS is a stale binding — the model failing closed because it no longer trusts its own classification. Ignorance reaches HUMAN_OWNS; obligation never does.

An action that creates a legally binding commitment — signing an NDA, filing a chargeback, releasing escrow — is graded SUPERVISED or APPROVAL_REQUIRED, exactly like any other reversible action. The commitment is invisible to the gate, because "does this bind a party?" is not a question the model can ask.

